ed to `filteredTodos` regardless of which status filter is selected —\\non paper it should already apply once \\\"Backlog\\\" is selected. However, I found\\none concrete, reproducible defect in that same method that plausibly explains\\ninconsistent behaviour (including for backlog) and should be ...\n  Line 329: {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-sonnet-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cecwjwds6Q6wxpLr5hHch\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I've completed the analysis and written the task breakdown to `docs/jobs/effort_hide-it-should-apply-to-backlog/tasks.md`. Summary:\\n\\n**Branch check:** confirmed the worktree is on `feature/effort_hide-it-should-apply-to-backlog`, matching `brief.md`.\\n\\n**Findings:** I traced the \\\"Hide It\\\" logic (`Todos.svelte.ts` → `useFilters()`, `TodoFilterService.filterByHideIt`/`filterOutBacklog`). On paper, hide-it already applies to the backlog view in the simple case — but I found one concrete, reproducible defect: `useFilters()` checks `isCategoryFilterActive()`/`isTagFilterActive()` against `this.activeFilters`, which is only updated to the new filter selection at the *end* of the function — so the hide-it guard can evaluate against the *previous* filter state (e.g. switching from a category filter to the \\\"Backlog\\\" status filter). I could not find a path where hide-it is unconditionally skipped for backlog in the simplest case, and I have no way to run the app or test suite from this role to confirm empirically.\\n\\n**Tasks produced:**\\n- TASK-1 (low risk): mandatory reproduction step before any fix, to confirm or rule out the stale-filter theory.\\n- TASK-2 (medium risk): fix `Todos.useFilters()` in `src/lib/state/Todos.svelte.ts`, depends on TASK-1.\\n- TASK-3 (low risk): add regression tests in `tests/unit/stores/Todos.test.ts`, depends on TASK-2.\\n\\nI flagged a separate, likely-unrelated observation (Kanban board never shows a backlog column) as o